\begin_layout Subsection

\lang brazilian
Newton's Second Law
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
A law of physics can be understood as a rule that describes—usually by means of a mathematical formulation—how certain physical systems behave(
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "Siddiqui2014"
literal "false"

\end_inset

). Newton's second law, for example, can be expressed by the equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
F\left(x,t\right)=m\frac{d^{2}}{dt^{2}}x\left(t\right)
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
where 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 represents the position of a particle of mass 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 relative to the origin at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

. When we solve this differential equation under certain initial conditions, we obtain as a solution a function 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

. Thus, once the forces acting on the particle and its initial conditions are specified, Newton's second law determines its temporal evolution: solving the equation provides us with the equation of motion of the particle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
For example, for a free particle(
\begin_inset Formula $F=0$
\end_inset

), we can obtain its equation of motion from Newton's second law:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
0=m\frac{d^{2}x}{dt^{2}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Defining the velocity as 
\begin_inset Formula $v\left(t\right)\equiv\frac{dx}{dt}$
\end_inset

, we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
0 & =\frac{d^{2}x}{dt^{2}}\\
0 & =\frac{dv}{dt}\\
0\cdot\int^{t}_{t_{0}}dt' & =\int^{t}_{t_{0}}\frac{dv}{dt'}dt'\\
0 & =v\left(t\right)-v\left(t_{0}\right)
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $v\left(t\right)=v\left(t_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

, that is, the particle's velocity is constant. Denoting this constant velocity by 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

, we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
v\left(t\right) & =v\left(t_{0}\right)\\
\frac{dx}{dt} & =v\\
\int^{t}_{t_{0}}\frac{dx}{dt'}dt' & =v\int^{t}_{t_{0}}dt'\\
x\left(t\right)-x\left(t_{0}\right) & =v\left(t-t_{0}\right)
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t_{0}\right)=x_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{0}=0$
\end_inset

, we obtain:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
x\left(t\right)=x_{0}+vt
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
In this case, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 is easily interpretable as the position of the particle at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang brazilian
Schrödinger Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
The quantum mechanical analogue of Newton's second law is the Schrödinger equation. In one dimension and in the non-relativistic case, the Schrödinger equation is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi\left(x,t\right)=-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial x^{2}}\Psi\left(x,t\right)+V\left(x,t\right)\Psi\left(x,t\right)
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
The corresponding equation for a free particle (
\begin_inset Formula $V\left(x,t\right)=0$
\end_inset

) is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
i\hbar\frac{\partial\Psi}{\partial t} & =-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{\partial^{2}\Psi}{\partial x^{2}}
\end{align}
\end_inset

Using the method of separation of variables, that is, assuming that the solution can be separated into a spatial part 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 and a temporal part 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

, that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)=\Phi\left(x\right)\Pi\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

. Then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\left(\Phi\left(x\right)\Pi\left(t\right)\right) & =-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial x^{2}}\left(\Phi\left(x\right)\Pi\left(t\right)\right)\\
i\hbar\frac{1}{\Pi}\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial t} & =-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{1}{\Phi}\frac{\partial^{2}\Phi}{\partial x^{2}}
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Since one side depends only on the spatial dimension (
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

) and the other only on the temporal dimension (
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

), the only way this can be true is if both sides are equal to a constant: 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

. Solving then for the temporal part:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\frac{i\hbar}{\Pi}\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial t} & =E\\
\frac{d\Pi}{dt} & =-\frac{iE}{\hbar}\Pi
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Since the derivative of the function 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 is equal to the function itself multiplied by a constant, we have an exponential solution 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\left(t\right)=A\exp\left(-\frac{iE}{\hbar}t\right)$
\end_inset

. For the spatial part:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\frac{1}{\Phi}\frac{\partial^{2}\Phi}{\partial x^{2}} & =-\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}\\
\frac{\partial^{2}\Phi}{\partial x^{2}} & =-\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}\Phi
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Since this is a second-order linear homogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients, we can look for solutions of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(x\right)=e^{\pm ikx}$
\end_inset

. In this case, 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{ikx}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-ikx}$
\end_inset

 are two linearly independent solutions to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d^{2}\Phi}{dx^{2}}=-k^{2}\Phi$
\end_inset

. By the linearity of the equation, its general solution is a linear combination of these solutions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\Psi\left(x,t\right)=\Pi\left(t\right)\Phi\left(x\right)=A\exp\left(-\frac{iE}{\hbar}t\right)\left(B\exp\left(-i\sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}}x\right)+C\exp\left(i\sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}}x\right)\right)
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Or, writing 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{2}=2mE/\hbar^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega=\frac{E}{\hbar}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\Psi\left(x,t\right) & =K_{1}e^{-i\left(kx+\omega t\right)}+K_{2}e^{i\left(kx-\omega t\right)}
\end{align}
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{2}$
\end_inset

 are constants determined by the conditions imposed on the solution. Here, some questions already arise. When we solved Newton's second law, we obtained 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

, which described the position of the particle. When we solve the Schrödinger equation, we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

, but what is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

? What does 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

 describe?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
A somewhat more in-depth discussion of this can be found 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "here"
target "/dupla"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\lang brazilian
Postulates of quantum mechanics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang brazilian
Notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
To move forward, we need to know the postulates
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
A postulate is a proposition assumed to be true within a theory, serving as a starting point for the construction of its concepts and results.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of quantum mechanics. The first point to note is that they can be formulated in different ways from one book to another. We will use Professor Jonas Maziero's material (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "maziero"
literal "false"

\end_inset

). We need to begin, then, with some comments on Dirac notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
In this notation, we write a vector as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v\right\rangle $
\end_inset

, called a ket. Taking the conjugate transpose of this vector (or adjoint), we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle v\right|=\left|v\right\rangle ^{\dagger}=\left(\left|v\right\rangle ^{T}\right)^{*}$
\end_inset

, which is called a bra. If we represent the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 in an orthonormal basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|b_{j}\right\rangle \right\} $
\end_inset

, we can write it as a linear combination of the basis vectors: 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v\right\rangle =\sum_{i}\left\langle b_{i}|v\right\rangle \left|b_{i}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

. In matrix representation, we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left|v\right\rangle =\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \\
\vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle 
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}
\end_inset

The product of a bra and a ket is called the inner product and results in a scalar:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\langle w|v\right\rangle =\left|w\right\rangle ^{\dagger}\left|v\right\rangle =\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \\
\vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle 
\end{array}\right]=\sum^{n}_{j=1}\left\langle b_{j}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\left\langle b_{j}|v\right\rangle 
\end{equation}
\end_inset

On the other hand, the product of a ket and a bra is called the outer product and results in an operator, whose matrix representation is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left|v\right\rangle \left\langle w\right|=\left|v\right\rangle \left|w\right\rangle ^{\dagger}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \\
\vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle 
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\\
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
With this in mind, we can now move on to the postulates of quantum mechanics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\lang brazilian
State Postulate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\lang brazilian
In every physical theory, we seek to describe the possible states of the system of interest, the changes in these states (temporal evolution), and the observable quantities and their measurements. For example, in Newtonian Mechanics (NM), the state of a system at a certain instant of time is specified by the values of position and velocity relative to a certain inertial reference frame, and its temporal evolution is given by Newton's second law. The observables are essentially the positions, from which we obtain the velocities using the time parameter. It is implicit in NM that the values of the observables are determined even before they are measured, and that all these observables can have well-defined values at a given instant of time. Next, we will see the postulates of QM for describing states, changes in these states, observables, and their measurements. We will see that the measurement part differs greatly from that of NM, mainly because of the existence of observables that cannot have well-defined values at the same time (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "maziero"
literal "false"

\end_inset

). 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
The state of a quantum system is described by a normalized vector in a Hilbert space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

. For simplicity, we will consider 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 as a vector space equipped with an inner product function 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \cdot|\cdot\right\rangle :\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Furthermore, a Hilbert space is a complete complex vector space. To understand what it means to be complete, we first need to understand the concept of a Cauchy sequence. A Cauchy sequence is a sequence whose elements become arbitrarily close to one another as we advance through the sequence. A complete metric space
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
A metric space is, essentially, a set of objects in which we define a way of measuring the distance between two objects. In the case of a vector space with an inner product, the distance can be defined from the norm induced by the inner product.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is one in which every Cauchy sequence converges to a limit that also belongs to the space itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
By system, we refer to a degree of freedom
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
A degree of freedom corresponds to an independent physical variable that can vary and that can be used to characterize the state of a system. For example, the position and spin of a particle correspond to different degrees of freedom.
\end_layout

\end_inset

. For example, the spin of an electron is a system, another system would be its position, and so on. When we consider two or more degrees of freedom as a single entity, we call it a composite system, and if we divide the composite system into subsystems, its state is described by a normalized vector in a Hilbert space obtained by taking the tensor product of the individual spaces:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}=\mathcal{H}_{a}\otimes\mathcal{H}_{b}\otimes\dots
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\lang brazilian
Measurement Postulate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Observable (measurable) quantities are represented by linear Hermitian operators, 
\begin_inset Formula $O=O^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

, defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

. When we measure an observable, we will obtain one of its possible values, which are given by the eigenvalues of the Hermitian operator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Unlike classical mechanics (CM), quantum mechanics (QM), in general, does not allow us to predict with certainty the outcome of a measurement. What QM provides us with are the probabilities of obtaining the different outcomes, calculated by means of the Born rule:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(o|\psi\right)=\left|\left\langle \psi|o\right\rangle \right|^{2}=\left|\left\langle o|\psi\right\rangle \right|^{2}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
This is the probability of obtaining the value 
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

 when measuring the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

 of a system that is in the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\psi\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 immediately before the measurement, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|o\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 is the eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

 corresponding to the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

. 
\begin_inset Formula $O=O^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

 are defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

. When we measure an observable, we obtain one of its possible values, which are represented by the eigenvalues of the Hermitian operator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Before moving forward, we can stop to reflect for a moment. When we were working with classical mechanics and Newton's law, one of the observables of a particle was its position, and Newton's law directly described how this quantity varied in time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
However, when we approach quantum mechanics, the Schrödinger equation describes how the wave function varies in time (and space). With Dirac notation, as we will see when studying the dynamics postulate, we have a mathematical description of how the state vector of the system varies in time. However, the state vector is not itself an observable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Even temporarily ignoring the probabilistic aspect, we use the state vector to calculate quantities associated with observables. This already constitutes a fundamental difference between the classical and quantum descriptions of systems. But if we calculate scalar quantities associated with observables from the state vector (or the wave function) of the system, what is the physical meaning of the state vector itself? Does it even have a direct physical meaning?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
This is one of the points on which different interpretations of quantum mechanics diverge. There is still no consensus on which interpretation provides the correct description of physical reality, although some interpretations may be ruled out or considered problematic in light of certain theoretical arguments and experimental results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Likewise, different interpretations diverge on the question of whether all relevant physical information about a system is necessarily contained in its state vector. We will explore different interpretations later on. For now, it is enough to emphasize that the physical meaning of the state vector and the status of the information it contains are profound interpretational questions on which there is no consensus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\lang brazilian
Repeatability of measurements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
However, there is one more detail that, although it is not explicitly contained in the postulates we have presented so far, is significantly different when we are in the quantum domain: performing a measurement affects the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
When we observe the position of a soccer ball that moves according to classical mechanics, we can, in principle, make this observation without significantly altering the state of the ball. We can therefore consider that the observation was performed approximately “externally” to the system itself, leaving it practically unchanged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
In the quantum domain, however, the situation is different. When we perform a measurement, the system interacts with the measurement apparatus and, in general, its state is significantly altered. One consequence of this is that, if we measure the same observable twice immediately in succession, the result of the second measurement will necessarily be equal to that obtained in the first.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Indeed, suppose that the first measurement of the observable (
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

) produced the result (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

). After this measurement, the state of the system becomes a state associated with the result (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

). In the case where the eigenvalue (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

) is non-degenerate
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
A non-degenerate eigenvalue is one to which a single eigenvector corresponds, up to a global phase, that is, a complex phase factor 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{i\phi}$
\end_inset

 that multiplies the state vector and does not affect the measurement outcomes, since 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{i\phi}\left(e^{i\phi}\right)^{*}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

, we can write
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(o|\psi\right)=1,\quad\rightarrow\left|\psi\right\rangle =e^{i\phi}\left|o\right\rangle 
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
A second immediately subsequent measurement of the same observable will therefore once again produce the result (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Note that, theoretically, before the measurement is performed, quantum mechanics provides us only with the probabilities associated with the possible outcomes. In the laboratory, however, an individual realization of the measurement necessarily produces a definite outcome. This leads us to another fundamental question: the probabilistic nature of quantum mechanics. Its probabilistic predictions concern the frequencies and distributions of outcomes obtained from ensembles of systems prepared in equivalent ways and subjected to the same experimental procedure, and they do not, in general, determine which specific outcome will be obtained in an individual realization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Before the measurement is performed, we have only the probabilities associated with the different values we may obtain. In this situation, in what state is the system? When dealing with classical mechanics, deterministic dynamics allows us, in principle, to determine the state of a system at an earlier instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t'<t$
\end_inset

 by knowing its state at an instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

. Can we extend this line of reasoning to quantum mechanics? Is the probabilistic character of the theory a fundamental feature of nature, or merely an expression of our ignorance about the state of the system? Again, we have no conclusive answers to this question, and different interpretations of quantum mechanics offer different answers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
And finally, still related to the probabilistic nature of measurements, misguided interpretations often support the idea that quantum mechanics is a scientific formulation that naturally incorporates the possibility of free will. However, the probabilistic character of the theory, by itself, provides no support for this conclusion and does not make quantum mechanics compatible with free will. We can discuss this misconception in greater detail when we address the dynamics postulate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\lang brazilian
Quantum incompatibility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 be two Hermitian operators defined on a finite-dimensional Hilbert space of dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

. By the spectral theorem, we can write them in their respective spectral decompositions as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
A= & \sum^{d}_{j=1}a_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{j}\right| & B= & \sum^{d}_{j=1}b_{j}\left|b_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle b_{j}\right|
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
The numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{j}$
\end_inset

 are the respective eigenvalues, and the sets
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right\} ^{d}_{j=1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|b_{j}\right\rangle \right\} ^{d}_{j=1}$
\end_inset

 form orthonormal bases of eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, respectively, that is, they diagonalize these operators, so that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
A\left|a_{j}\right\rangle  & =a_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle  & B\left|b_{j}\right\rangle = & b_{j}\left|b_{j}\right\rangle 
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Assuming that the two operators share the same basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|a_{j}\right\rangle =\left|b_{j}\right\rangle \coloneqq\left|j\right\rangle $
\end_inset

, then, with the commutator defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA$
\end_inset

, we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\left[A,B\right] & =\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}a_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\right)\left(\sum^{d}_{k=1}b_{k}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|\right)-\left(\sum^{d}_{k=1}b_{k}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|\right)\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}a_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\right)\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}a_{j}b_{k}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j|k\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|-\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}b_{k}a_{j}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle k|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}a_{j}b_{k}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|\delta_{jk}-\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}b_{k}a_{j}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\delta_{jk}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\left(a_{j}b_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|-b_{j}a_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\right)\\
 & =0
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
0=\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA\Longrightarrow AB=BA
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Thus, we have that if the operators share the same set of eigenvectors, they commute. And to prove that if they commute, they share the same basis of eigenvectors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
A\left(B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right) & =\left(AB\right)\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \\
 & =B\left(A\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right)\\
 & =B\left(a_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right)\\
 & =a_{j}B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle 
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
That is, in this case, we must have that 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 belong to the eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 corresponding to the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is non-degenerate, its eigenspace has dimension 1. Therefore, any eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is linearly dependent on 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 also belongs to this eigenspace, we necessarily have 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle =b_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Now, if 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate, that is, if there are two or more linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the same eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

, then there exists a subspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 generated by the eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

. It is a little more complex, but it is the same idea applied to a subspace of dimension greater than 1. To understand this, we will base our discussion on another source (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "MIT804_CommonEigenbases"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
For the degenerate case, we then have more vectors associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

. Let us call this subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 and, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle \right\} ^{d_{j}}_{k=1}$
\end_inset

 being any basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle =a_{j}\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

. Now, since 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \in\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

, that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 transforms vectors from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 into vectors that also belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

. In particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 transforms an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 into a vector that remains in the same eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle \neq0$
\end_inset

, this vector is also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Therefore, each 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 can be written as a linear combination of the basis vectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

. Thus, we can consider the action of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 “internally” within the subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian, its restriction to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 is also Hermitian. And since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 has finite dimension (and is Hermitian), we can diagonalize this restriction of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 in an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

. Therefore, there exists a basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

, which we can denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle \right\} $
\end_inset

, such that 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle =b_{j}\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

. Since this new basis remains a basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

, its vectors continue to be eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Thus, the eigenvectors are simultaneously eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

. Repeating this procedure for each eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, we obtain an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 consisting of common eigenvectors of the two operators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
In other words, if the Hermitian operators 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute, then they share the same orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. To better understand this proof, we can represent the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 as a matrix in the basis of eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

. Using the completeness relation, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{I}=\sum_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{j}\right|$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $B=\mathbb{I}B\mathbb{I}=\sum_{j,k}\left(\left\langle a_{j}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle \right)\left(\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{k}\right|\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Thus, the elements of the matrix representing 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 in this basis are 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{a_{j},a_{k}}=\left\langle a_{j}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

, or in matrix form:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
B=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle a_{1}\left|B\right|a_{1}\right\rangle  & \left\langle a_{1}\left|B\right|a_{2}\right\rangle  & \dots\\
\left\langle a_{2}\left|B\right|a_{1}\right\rangle  & \left\langle a_{2}\left|B\right|a_{2}\right\rangle  & \dots\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Then we can think of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 as a block-diagonal matrix:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \[
B=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
B_{1} & 0 & 0\\
0 & B_{2} & 0\\
0 & 0 & \ddots
\end{array}\right]
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
where each 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a submatrix representing the internal action of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 acting on the subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

. If the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is non-degenerate, then the block 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is just a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times1$
\end_inset

 matrix. If 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate, then the block 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 has dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{j}\times d_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian, each block 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is also a Hermitian matrix and, therefore, can be diagonalized. Thus, we can diagonalize 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 within each subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

, obtaining a basis of eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 that are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

. Thus, we prove that if and only if two operators 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute, then they share a common basis of eigenvectors. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Example 1"
target "/exemplo-incompatibilidade"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
In summary, when we have a non-degenerate eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

, the situation is very simple, since in this case, the only eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, up to a multiplicative factor, is automatically also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
When 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate, its eigenvectors form a subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

, corresponding to the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

, in which every nonzero vector is also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute, applying 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to any vector in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 results in another vector belonging to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

. Thus, it is sufficient to find, within this subspace, the vectors that are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
This possibility is guaranteed by the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian: its action restricted to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 can be represented by a Hermitian submatrix, which can be diagonalized by an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
The fact that a matrix is Hermitian guarantees that we can choose, in the space on which it acts, an orthonormal basis formed by its eigenvectors (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "DelftSpectralTheorem"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "on this page"
target "https://interactivetextbooks.tudelft.nl/mqp-v2/operators.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\end_inset

. Therefore, we can choose an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 formed by eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

. Since all vectors in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

, these vectors are common eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Repeating this procedure for each eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, we obtain an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 formed by common eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
With the proof of this theorem, we know that if two observables 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 of a system do not commute, they cannot share all eigenvectors. If, by measuring the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, we prepare the system in a state that is not an eigenvector of the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, this vector must necessarily be a linear combination of the eigenvectors of with two or more nonzero probability amplitudes. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Example 2"
target "/exemplo-incompatibilidade"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\lang brazilian
Uncertainty Relation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Here we will use the quantum kinematic formalism to obtain general mathematical relations that provide limitations on the product or sum of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

uncertainties
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (standard deviations) of two observables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\series bold
Heisenberg-Robertson-Schrödinger Uncertainty Relation (HRSUR)
\series default

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
The HRSUR : 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right)\ge(\text{CovQ}\left(A,B\right))^{2}+2^{-2}|\langle[A,B]\rangle|^{2},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
with the variance defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Var}(A)=\langle(A-\langle A\rangle)^{2}\rangle.$
\end_inset

 The quantities on the right-hand side of the inequality will be defined below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
To prove the HRSUR, we will use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality (CSI) as one of the main tools:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\langle\psi|\psi\rangle\langle\phi|\phi\rangle\ge|\langle\psi|\phi\rangle|^{2}=\langle\psi|\phi\rangle\langle\phi|\psi\rangle,
\end{equation}
\end_inset

with equality obtained if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $|\phi\rangle$
\end_inset

 are collinear (
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle\propto|\phi\rangle$
\end_inset

). This result is valid for any pair of vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle,|\phi\rangle$
\end_inset

 in a Hilbert space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}.$
\end_inset

 Consider 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to be two observables of a physical system prepared in the state 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi\rangle$
\end_inset

. We will use
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \[
\langle X\rangle:=\langle X\rangle_{\xi}=\langle\xi|X|\xi\rangle
\]
\end_inset

to denote the expectation value of any operator 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 to denote the identity operator in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

. Before proceeding, I would like to explore this result further. Let us consider a system in the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\psi\right\rangle =\sum_{n}c_{n}\left|\psi\right\rangle $
\end_inset

, and an operator 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 with eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{n}$
\end_inset

 associated with the eigenvectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|x_{n}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

. By the definition of a weighted average, the mean value of the results is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle O\right\rangle =\sum_{n}o_{n}P\left(o_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

, and since 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(o_{n}\right)=\left|c_{n}\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

, we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle O\right\rangle =\sum_{n}o_{n}\left|c_{n}\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle o_{n}|\psi\right\rangle =c_{n}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle O\right\rangle =\sum_{n}o_{n}\left\langle \psi|o_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle o_{n}|\psi\right\rangle $
\end_inset

, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\left\langle \psi|o_{n}\right\rangle \right|=\sqrt{\left\langle o_{n}|\psi\right\rangle \left\langle \psi|o_{n}\right\rangle }$
\end_inset

. Then, by the spectral decomposition 
\begin_inset Formula $O=\sum_{n}o_{n}\left|o_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle o_{n}\right|$
\end_inset

, we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle O\right\rangle =\left\langle \psi\right|\left(\sum_{n}o_{n}\left|o_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle o_{n}\right|\right)\left|\psi\right\rangle =\left\langle \psi\left|o\right|\psi\right\rangle 
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
We will also define the following vectors: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
|\psi\rangle & =(A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle,\\
|\phi\rangle & =(B-\langle B\rangle I)|\xi\rangle,
\end{align}
\end_inset

which we will replace at CSI. Let us note that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\langle\psi|\psi\rangle & =\left((A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle,(A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle\right)\\
 & =\left(|\xi\rangle,(A-\langle A\rangle I)^{\dagger}(A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle\right)\\
 & =\langle\xi|(A-\langle A\rangle I)^{2}|\xi\rangle\\
 & =\mathrm{Var}(A),\\
\langle\phi|\phi\rangle & =\mathrm{Var}(B),
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Var}(X)=\mathrm{Var}(X)_{\xi}=\langle\xi|(X-\langle X\rangle I)^{2}|\xi\rangle=\langle(X-\langle X\rangle I)^{2}\rangle$
\end_inset

 being the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

. We also use 
\begin_inset Formula $(A-\langle A\rangle I)^{\dagger}(A-\langle A\rangle I)=(A-\langle A\rangle I)^{2}$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $A=A^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle =\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*}$
\end_inset

. We can see that this follows from the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 (and 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

) are Hermitian, and: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*} & =\left(\left\langle \psi\left|A\right|\psi\right\rangle \right)^{*}=\left\langle \psi\left|A^{\dagger}\right|\psi\right\rangle =\left\langle \psi\left|A\right|\psi\right\rangle =\left\langle A\right\rangle 
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Furthermore, we have to 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\langle\psi|\phi\rangle & =((A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle,(B-\langle B\rangle I)|\xi\rangle)\\
 & =(|\xi\rangle,(A-\langle A\rangle I)(B-\langle B\rangle I)|\xi\rangle)\\
 & =(|\xi\rangle,(AB-A\langle B\rangle-\langle A\rangle B+\langle A\rangle\langle B\rangle I)|\xi\rangle)\\
 & =\left\langle \xi\left|\left(AB-A\langle B\rangle-\langle A\rangle B+\langle A\rangle\langle B\rangle I\right)\right|\xi\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle \xi\left|AB\right|\xi\right\rangle -\left\langle \xi\left|A\right|\xi\right\rangle \left\langle \xi\left|B\right|\xi\right\rangle -\left\langle \xi\left|A\right|\xi\right\rangle \left\langle \xi\left|B\right|\xi\right\rangle +\left\langle \xi\left|A\right|\xi\right\rangle \left\langle \xi\left|BI\right|\xi\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle AB\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle +\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle AB\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle 
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
And since: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\left[A,B\right] & =AB-BA,\\
\left\{ A,B\right\}  & =AB+BA
\end{align}
\end_inset

are, respectively, the commutator and anticommutator of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

. Summing them:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\left[A,B\right]+\left\{ A,B\right\}  & =AB-BA+AB+BA\\
 & =2AB\\
2^{-1}\left(\left[A,B\right]+\left\{ A,B\right\} \right) & =AB
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula \[
AB=2^{-1}(\{A,B\}+[A,B]).
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle  & =\left\langle AB\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle 2^{-1}(\{A,B\}+[A,B])\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \\
 & =2^{-1}\left(\left\langle \{A,B\}\right\rangle +\left\langle [A,B]\right\rangle \right)-\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle 
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
So far, then, we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\left\langle \psi|\psi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi|\phi\right\rangle  & \ge\left|\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right|{}^{2}\\
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\left|2^{-1}\left(\left\langle \{A,B\}\right\rangle +\left\langle [A,B]\right\rangle \right)-\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \right|{}^{2}
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\{A,B\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $[A,B]$
\end_inset

 are, respectively, Hermitian (
\begin_inset Formula $\{A,B\}^{\dagger}=\{A,B\}$
\end_inset

) and anti-Hermitian (
\begin_inset Formula $[A,B]^{\dagger}=-[A,B]$
\end_inset

) operators, their expectation values are, respectively, purely real and purely imaginary numbers. We have already verified that the expectation value of a Hermitian operator is a real number; now, for an anti-Hermitian operator:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*} & =\left(\left\langle \psi\left|A\right|\psi\right\rangle \right)^{*}=\left\langle \psi\left|A^{\dagger}\right|\psi\right\rangle =-\left\langle \psi\left|A\right|\psi\right\rangle =-\left\langle A\right\rangle 
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Now, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle =a+ib$
\end_inset

, its conjugate 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*}=a-ib$
\end_inset

, and if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*}=-\left\langle A\right\rangle $
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $a+ib=-\left(a-ib\right)$
\end_inset

, that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $a+ib=-a+ib$
\end_inset

. For this to be equal, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $a=0$
\end_inset

. Thus, we are left with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle =ib$
\end_inset

. Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle B\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 are also real numbers, we can verify this for 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A,B\right\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\left(A,B\right) & =AB\mp BA\\
\left(A,B\right)^{\dagger} & =\left(AB\mp BA\right)^{\dagger}\\
 & =\left(AB\right)^{\dagger}\mp\left(BA\right)^{\dagger}\\
 & =B^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}\mp A^{\dagger}B^{\dagger}\\
 & =\mp A^{\dagger}B^{\dagger}+B^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}
\end{align*}
\end_inset

That is, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]^{\dagger}=-\left[A,B\right]$
\end_inset

. And if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A,B\right\} =AB+BA$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A,B\right\} ^{\dagger}=\left\{ A,B\right\} $
\end_inset

. Thus, we can write:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle  & =\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)+i\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)\\
 & =\left[2^{-1}\left(\left\langle \left\{ A,B\right\} \right\rangle \right)-\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \right]+\left[2^{-1}\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right]
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
We then have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\Re(\langle\psi|\phi\rangle) & =2^{-1}\langle\{A,B\}-2\langle A\rangle\langle B\rangle\mathbb{I}\rangle,\\
\Im(\langle\psi|\phi\rangle) & =-i2^{-1}\langle[A,B]\rangle.
\end{align}
\end_inset

Note: Since 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{-1}\langle[A,B]\rangle=ib$
\end_inset

 is a purely imaginary number, multiplying by 
\begin_inset Formula $-i$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $-i\cdot\left(ib\right)=b$
\end_inset

, gives us its real coefficient. Thus, considering that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\left|\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right|^{2} & =\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle ^{*}\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \\
 & =\left(\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)-i\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)\right)\left(\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)+i\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)\right)\\
 & =\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}+\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
We then have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\mathrm{Var}(A)\mathrm{Var}(B) & \geq(\Re(\langle\psi|\phi\rangle))^{2}+(\Im(\langle\psi|\phi\rangle))^{2}
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Now let's open it: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}=\left(-i2^{-1}\langle[A,B]\rangle\right)^{2}=-2^{-2}\langle[A,B]\rangle^{2}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Furthermore, since it is purely imaginary, then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \[
\langle[A,B]\rangle^{2}=\left(-ib\right)^{2}=-b^{2}=-\left|b\right|^{2}=-\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
We are then left with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}=2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}$
\end_inset

. And our inequality:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\left\langle \psi|\psi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi|\phi\right\rangle  & \ge\left|\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right|{}^{2}\\
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}+\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}\\
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}+2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
It now remains for us to demonstrate that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \[
\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}=CovQ\left(A,B\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{Cov\left(A,B\right)+Cov\left(BA\right)}{2}\right)^{2}
\]
\end_inset

Where the covariance is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)=\langle XY\rangle-\langle X\rangle\langle Y\rangle$
\end_inset

 between the observables 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

, therefore:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2} & =\left(\frac{\langle AB\rangle-\langle A\rangle\langle B\rangle+\langle BA\rangle-\langle B\rangle\langle A\rangle}{2}\right)^{2}\\
\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2} & =\left(\frac{\langle AB\rangle+\langle BA\rangle-2\langle B\rangle\langle A\rangle}{2}\right)^{2}\\
 & =\left(\frac{\left\langle AB+BA\right\rangle }{2}-\langle B\rangle\langle A\rangle\right)^{2}\\
 & =\left(2^{-1}\left\langle \left\{ AB\right\} \right\rangle -\langle B\rangle\langle A\rangle\right)^{2}
\end{align*}
\end_inset

And this 
\begin_inset Formula $\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)=2^{-1}\left\langle \left\{ A,B\right\} \right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 is the result we had obtained. We can then write 
\begin_inset Formula $\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}=\text{CovQ}\left(A,B\right)^{2}$
\end_inset

 and:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}+2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}\\
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\text{CovQ}\left(A,B\right)^{2}+2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
It is worth noting that if 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 are compatible observables, i.e., if 
\begin_inset Formula $[A,B]=0$
\end_inset

, then we will have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{CovQ}(A,B)=\mathrm{Cov}(A,B)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Let us now analyze the problem of the possible triviality of the HRSUR, whose general objective is to determine how small the product of the variances of a pair of observables can be. For this, let us assume that the system is prepared in a state that is an eigenvector of the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \[
|\xi\rangle=|a\rangle
\]
\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $A|a\rangle=a|a\rangle$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

. In this case, it can be verified that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathrm{Var}(A)_{|a\rangle}=\mathrm{CovQ}(A,B)_{|a\rangle}=\langle[A,B]\rangle_{|a\rangle}=0.
\end{equation}
\end_inset

And with that, HRSUR gets back to us:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
0\mathrm{Var}(B)\ge0.
\end{equation}
\end_inset

Therefore, in this case, the HRSUR provides no information whatsoever about the possible incompatibility of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

. The same type of limitation applies if 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi\rangle=|b\rangle$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $B|b\rangle=b|b\rangle.$
\end_inset

 In any case, let us verify. For the commutator:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\left\langle a_{k}\left|\left[A,B\right]\right|a_{k}\right\rangle  & =\left\langle a_{k}\left|AB\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -\left\langle a_{k}\left|BA\right|a_{k}\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle a_{k}\left|\sum_{n}a_{n}\left|a_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{n}\right|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\sum_{n}a_{n}\left|a_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{n}\right|\right|a_{k}\right\rangle \\
 & =\sum_{n}a_{n}\left[\left\langle a_{k}|a_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{n}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{n}|a_{k}\right\rangle \right]\\
 & =a_{k}\left[\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle \right]\\
 & =0
\end{align*}
\end_inset

For the variance, we will adopt a different strategy: instead of writing out the spectral decomposition, we will use the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $A|a\rangle=a|a\rangle$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\mathrm{Var}(A)_{|a\rangle} & =\langle a_{n}|(A-\langle a_{n}\left|A\right|a_{n}\rangle I)^{2}|a_{n}\rangle\\
 & =\langle a_{n}|(A-a_{n}\langle a_{n}|a_{n}\rangle I)^{2}|a_{n}\rangle\\
 & =\langle a_{n}|(A-a_{n}I)^{2}|a_{n}\rangle\\
 & =\langle a_{n}|A^{2}|a_{n}\rangle+a^{2}_{n}\langle a_{n}|a_{n}\rangle-2a_{n}\langle a_{n}|A|a_{n}\rangle\\
 & =a^{2}_{n}+a^{2}_{n}-2a^{2}_{n}\\
 & =0
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
And for Quantum Covariance, we've already seen that 
\begin_inset Formula $\langle a_{n}\left|A\right|a_{n}\rangle=a_{n}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle a_{k}\left|AB\right|a_{k}\right\rangle =\left\langle a_{k}\left|BA\right|a_{k}\right\rangle =a_{k}\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

, then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\mathrm{CovQ}(A,B)_{|a\rangle} & =\frac{\langle a_{k}\left|AB\right|a_{k}\rangle+\langle a_{k}\left|BA\right|a_{k}\rangle-2\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\rangle\langle a_{k}\left|A\right|a_{k}\rangle}{2}\\
 & =\frac{a_{k}\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle +a_{k}\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -2a_{k}\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\rangle}{2}\\
 & =0
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\series bold
Robertson and Heisenberg Uncertainty Relations (HUR)
\series default

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Ignoring
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed


\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
That is, if 
\begin_inset Formula $a\geq b+c\geq c$
\end_inset

. With 
\begin_inset Formula $b\geq0$
\end_inset

, then necessarily 
\begin_inset Formula $a\geq c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 the term related to quantum covariance in the HRSUR and taking the square root of the remaining expression, we obtain the Robertson uncertainty relation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\text{CovQ}\left(A,B\right)^{2}+2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}\\
\sqrt{\text{Var}\left(A\right)}\sqrt{\text{Var}\left(B\right)} & \geq\sqrt{2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}}\\
\sigma_{A}\sigma_{B} & \ge2^{-1}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
with, e.g., 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{A}=\sqrt{Var(A)}.$
\end_inset

 When applied to position and linear momentum, it is one of Heisenberg's uncertainty relations: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\sigma_{X}\sigma_{P}\ge2^{-1}|\langle[X,P]\rangle|=\hbar/2,
\end{equation}
\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $[X,P]=i\hbar I$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Here we are in a position to engage in some further discussion. Heisenberg's uncertainty relation is constantly the subject of misunderstandings. The first concerns the status of the relation itself. Although it is traditionally called an uncertainty principle, it does not need to be postulated as a principle. As we have seen, Heisenberg's relation can be obtained as a particular case of the Heisenberg-Robertson-Schrödinger Uncertainty Relation, which, in turn, can be derived from the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality, together with the mathematical properties of observables represented by operators in a Hilbert space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
The second point is that it is incorrect to interpret the uncertainty relation as stating that a particle 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

does not have a definite position and momentum
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

we cannot measure position and momentum in the same preparation
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

. The first statement is a possible interpretation, but it is not the mathematical content of the relation, whereas the second is simply incorrect. The uncertainty relation does not say that it is impossible to obtain position and momentum results in the same preparation; it establishes a relationship between the variances of the results of these measurements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
In other words, we can repeatedly prepare the system in the same state and perform position and momentum measurements. From the results, we can determine the respective distributions and, consequently, their variances. The uncertainty relation establishes that the product of these variances — or, more precisely, of the standard deviations — must satisfy a given relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
This contrasts with classical mechanics. We can imagine, for example, a particle at rest at a perfectly determined position. Ignoring experimental limitations, it would be possible to conceive of a classical state in which position and velocity were simultaneously well defined, for example, a particle at rest at the origin (
\begin_inset Formula $x=0,v=0$
\end_inset

). In this case, if we repeated the measurements, we would always obtain the same values and, therefore, we would have 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}=\sigma_{v}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang brazilian
Dynamics Postulate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Changes of states in QM are described by a unitary transformation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
|\psi_{t}\rangle=U_{t}|\psi_{0}\rangle,
\end{equation}
\end_inset

i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula \[
U_{t}U^{\dagger}_{t}=U_{t}U^{\dagger}_{t}=\mathbb{I}.
\]
\end_inset

The time-evolution operator being obtained from the Schrödinger equation: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t}=H_{t}U_{t},
\end{equation}
\end_inset

with the initial condition: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
U_{0}=\mathbb{I},
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
where 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the Hamiltonian operator of the system at the instant of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar$
\end_inset

 is Planck's constant divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $2\pi$
\end_inset

 and we use the notation 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{t}=\frac{\partial}{\partial_{t}}$
\end_inset

. The two equations above are equivalent to the Schrödinger equation for the state 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
i\hbar\partial_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle=H_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle.
\end{equation}
\end_inset

We verify this result as follows: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
i\hbar\partial_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle & =i\hbar\partial_{t}(U_{t}|\psi_{0}\rangle)\\
 & =(i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t})|\psi_{0}\rangle\\
 & =H_{t}U_{t}|\psi_{0}\rangle\\
 & =H_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle.
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\series bold
Example
\series default
: When 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{t}=H$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
In this type of situation—i.e., when the Hamiltonian is time-independent—we can verify that the following general form holds: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
U_{t}=e^{-iHt/\hbar}.
\end{equation}
\end_inset

We will do this first using the spectral decomposition of the Hamiltonian, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
H=\sum_{j}E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|,
\]
\end_inset

and applying the operator function: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t} & =i\hbar\partial_{t}\exp\left(-\frac{iHt}{\hbar}\right)\\
 & =i\hbar\partial_{t}\exp\left(\sum_{j}\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)
\end{align}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Here we should pause to understand the mathematics. We have seen that for any Hermitian operator 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(T)=\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{1}{n!}T^{n}$
\end_inset

. First, we can see that 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
H^{2} & =\left(\sum_{j}E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)\left(\sum_{n}E_{n}|E_{n}\rangle\langle E_{n}|\right)=\sum_{j,n}E_{j}E_{n}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|E_{n}\rangle\langle E_{n}|=\sum_{j}E^{2}_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|
\end{align*}
\end_inset

where we used the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle E_{j}|E_{n}\right\rangle =\delta_{jn}$
\end_inset

. In general, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{n}=\sum_{j}E^{n}_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|$
\end_inset

. Writing then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
\exp(H) & =\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{1}{n!}H^{n}\\
 & =\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{1}{n!}\left(\sum_{j}E^{n}_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)\\
 & =\sum_{j}\left(\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{E^{n}_{j}}{n!}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|
\end{align*}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
And since 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(x)=\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{x^{n}}{n!}$
\end_inset

, then: 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(H)=\sum_{j}e^{E_{j}}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|$
\end_inset

. In our case, we then have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian

\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t} & =i\hbar\sum_{j}\partial_{t}\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\\
 & =i\hbar\sum_{j}\left(-\frac{iE_{j}}{\hbar}\right)\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\\
 & =i\hbar\left(-\frac{i}{\hbar}\right)\sum_{j}\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\\
 & =\sum_{j}e^{-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}}H|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}\\
 & =H\left(\sum_{j}e^{-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)
\end{align}
\end_inset

where we used 
\begin_inset Formula $H|E_{j}\rangle=E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle$
\end_inset

 throughout the entire proof. But now, since 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{j}e^{-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|=e^{-\frac{it}{\hbar}\sum_{j}E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|}=e^{-\frac{iHt}{\hbar}|}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t}=He^{-\frac{iHt}{\hbar}|}$
\end_inset

 and since 
\begin_inset Formula $i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t}=H_{t}U_{t},$
\end_inset

 therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{t}=e^{-iHt/\hbar}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
OBS. Note that we could obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{t}$
\end_inset

 by solving the Schrödinger equation for each Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

. However, having this general formula, we can begin the solution by calculating the exponential directly, without having to solve the differential equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Now we can address another question related to “determinism” as opposed to free will. There are at least two senses of the word determinism. One is more closely associated with classical physics and consists of the idea that, if we know perfectly the current state of a system and the laws governing its evolution, its future and past states are, in principle, determined. The other is a broader sense, of a philosophical nature, which concerns the denial of free will.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Regarding the first sense, the usual formulation of quantum mechanics introduces a probabilistic element that contrasts with the determinism of classical mechanics. The results of measurements are, in general, not uniquely determined by the previous quantum state, but are described by probability distributions. In this sense, the theory contains an indeterministic element. It is worth noting, however, that the Schrödinger equation governing the evolution of the quantum state is deterministic. Given the initial state and the Hamiltonian of the system, the equation uniquely determines the quantum state at any subsequent time. The probabilities of measurement outcomes are then obtained from this state through the Born rule.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Whether nature is ultimately deterministic and the probabilistic nature of quantum mechanics results from some incomplete description, or whether quantum indeterminacy is fundamental, is a question for which we do not have a consensual answer. Different positions regarding this question give rise, in part, to different interpretations of quantum mechanics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
However, it is a mistake to think that this probabilistic mathematical element would, by itself, serve as support for a defense of free will. There is no formulation in the known laws of physics that incorporates free will. We can approximate the chance of obtaining heads or tails when tossing an approximately symmetric coin to 50% for each outcome. This is a probabilistic event, but we would not say, for that reason, that the coin possesses free will. The introduction of probabilities into the description of nature, therefore, does not confer any more free will upon nature than the probabilistic tossing of a coin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang brazilian
Furthermore, not only is the evolution of the quantum state deterministic, but the probability distribution itself at any given instant imposes constraints on the possible outcomes and their probabilities. In my view, these constraints are incompatible with a conception of “free” will in which the agent should possess unrestricted freedom to determine any of the possible outcomes. 
\end_layout

